[2026-07-31 12:42:33] ← 23364655669309: ## Read of the deal before you plan the call

Two calls in, this is much further along than it feels. Here's what you actually have.

**They have already sold themselves.** Sajjad's response to the audit wasn't defensive, it was "you're absolutely correct, absolutely correct." Then he handed you the proof himself: the one time he wrote about Dinar Standard, a year ago, it got massive engagement. He has personally validated your thesis with his own data. That is as close to a pre-close as you get.

**They told you the decision criteria.** Sajjad: *"You need to force some decisions before you put a lot more time in."* He is asking you to close him. Most prospects hide that. He said it out loud on a recording.

**Money is not the objection.** They target $3-5M revenue, want 70-80% from retainers, and quoted anchor accounts at $150-300K per year. Their easy-land engagement is a $10-20K financial model. $3K a month against that is not a budget conversation, it's a conviction conversation. Do not let it become a budget conversation by treating it like one.

**The real blocker is a commitment decision between two brothers, not a vendor decision.** Sajjad said it plainly: if this works, Trivium becomes full-time, and that forces choices about GuardianCare, the finance platform, the governance platform. He is not deciding whether to hire you. He is deciding whether to bet on Trivium. That's the thing you have to make safe.

**Haseeb is the majority owner.** He said so at 21:00 on the 22nd. Sajjad is minority and is the face. That means Haseeb signs and Sajjad performs. If Haseeb isn't on the call, don't run it.

**Their funnel shape is perfect for you and you haven't said it out loud yet.** They close on one or two warm intros. Their conversion is fine. Nothing enters the top. That is precisely the problem content fixes, and precisely why their GTM has been flat for six years despite never having an angry client.

---

## Who needs what

**Sajjad** needs permission. He has spent two calls circling the same discomfort: influencer culture in the Muslim community, image without substance, people who say "message me" and never reply, Saudi projecting a reality that isn't real. He is 50, has 20 years of unrecorded work, and told you he's in a building phase. He needs to hear that going online is the opposite of what he's afraid of, not a compromise with it. If you raise this objection before he does, you win the call.

**Haseeb** needs the commercial case and cover on his employer risk. He works at an Insight-owned company, one rung below VP, and already proposed his own workaround: post about the Trivium team, he reposts. He is pro-content and pragmatic. He will move if the numbers work and his exposure is zero.

---

## Pre-call setup

Do all of this before you dial in.

**Send the deck now, not on the call.** Sajjad asked for it twice. Withholding it costs you goodwill and gives them a reason to defer. Send it with a short note that sets the frame:

> Sajjad, Haseeb, deck attached with the full profile analysis. Last time you said we should force some decisions before putting more time in. Agreed. So Tuesday isn't another strategy session. You'll have had the deck for four days. I'll spend the hour on your questions and objections, and by the end I'd like a yes or a no. Both work for me. Need you both on, at a desk if possible, 8am your time.

**Get 60 minutes at 8:00 or 8:30 ET.** He named that window twice as his best. Confirm in the WhatsApp group the night before.

**Have these open on your screen before the call starts:**
- Services agreement, pre-filled with Trivium's details, $3,000/month, rolling monthly
- Stripe payment link, live and tested
- Your calendar with three 90-minute onboarding slots in the next 10 days
- The three-number slide (100 / 6 / 0)

**Insist both are at a desk.** Sajjad was driving last time and dropped out of his own strategy call. This is a fair thing to ask for a decision meeting.

---

## The call, minute by minute

### 0-3: Lock the frame

Don't open with pleasantries beyond salaam and one line. Then:

> Sajjad, you said something last time that stuck with me. You said we should force some decisions before putting a lot more time in. So that's what this is. You've had the deck since Friday. I'm not going to re-present strategy at you. I want to spend this hour on every reason you might say no, and at the end I'm going to ask you directly. If it's a no, that's completely fine and we'll shake hands as friends of Samad. What I don't want is a third call where we agree it's a good idea. Does that work for both of you?

Get a verbal yes from each of them. That yes is the whole call. Everything after it is you cashing it.

### 3-10: Re-anchor the gap, then stop talking

Three numbers, sixty seconds. 100 posts. 6 in your own voice. 0 about Trivium.

Then the one thing they gave you:

> The part that stayed with me is what you said after. That the one time you did write about Dinar Standard, it went off. You already ran the experiment. It worked. Then you stopped for a year.

Then one line of diagnosis and stop:

> You're not missing a strategy. You've got twenty years of material sitting in your head. Wall Street, Dinar Standard, fourteen years in Saudi, PIF before MBS, the family office. None of it is on the page. That's the entire problem and it's a very fixable one.

Do not go into arcs, phases, TechCrunch, or Amjad Masad. You already did that. Repeating it burns your objection time.

### 10-16: Tie it to their number

This is the piece you haven't done yet and it's what Haseeb needs.

> Something I noticed from your call with Mustafa. You said deals close on one or two warm introductions, and that in six years you've never had an angry client. So your conversion isn't the problem. Nothing is entering the top. You told us the GTM has been flat, that it's all been inbound and word of mouth. That's not a positioning problem you fix with a better deck. It's a distribution problem.
>
> Your target is $3-5M with 70-80% recurring. That's roughly two or three anchor accounts at $150-300K plus a base of smaller mandates. So the question isn't whether this pays for itself. One additional anchor client covers four years of working with us. The question is whether you believe Sajjad writing his actual story on LinkedIn produces one warm introduction over twelve months. Does that resonate?

Then the proof, briefly:

> Cur8 is the closest comparison we have. Same shape of buyer, trust-heavy, decisions that take months, an audience that's Muslim and non-Muslim at once. £3M in tracked sales over nine months, 39 investors converted, £5M+ into AUM attributed to the content. Ibrahim had exactly your reservation about taste, by the way. We'll come to that.

That last sentence sets up the objection you're about to raise for him.

### 16-42: Surface every objection yourself

This is the bulk of the call and the part that wins it. Open it like this:

> Before I ask you to decide, I want to put every reason you might say no on the table. Including the ones you haven't said out loud. I'd rather hear them now than in a WhatsApp message in three weeks.

Then go through them by name. Raise the hard one first.

---

## Objection bank

### 1. "I don't want to become one of these influencers" (Sajjad, the real one)

Say it before he does. That is the whole move.

> Let me start with the one you raised with Mustafa, because I think it's the most important thing either of you said across both calls. You watched influencer culture take over the Muslim community for twenty years and you think it's corrosive. Image without ilm. People who say they're accessible and then don't reply. You spent fourteen years in a place where the projection and the reality didn't match, and it left a taste in your mouth.
>
> I want to be direct with you. I agree with you, and that's exactly why I want to work with you.
>
> The problem you're describing isn't that people post. It's that the people with nothing behind them post constantly, and the people with twenty years of real work behind them say nothing. That's the asymmetry. Every time someone serious opts out, the space you're worried about gets handed to someone less serious. You are not choosing between participating and staying clean. You're choosing between your voice being in that space and someone else's filling it.
>
> The second thing. You said if someone claims they're accessible and doesn't reply, that's a bad look. You're right, and that's a writing decision, not an inevitability. In onboarding we'll write a list of things we never post for you. No fake accessibility. No manufactured vulnerability. No selfie streams. You approve everything before it goes out, and if something feels off in month one, we kill it and recalibrate. Ibrahim at Cur8 had the same reservation. Two years in, nothing has gone out that he's regretted.

Then close the loop with a question, not a statement:

> So my question back to you. If we could put out content that your shaykhs could read without wincing, would you still not want to do it?

If he says no, you have him. If he hesitates, ask what specifically would make him wince, and write it down live. Writing it down in front of him is worth more than any argument.

### 2. "We need to align internally on how committed we are"

This is the one that killed the last call. Handle it by shrinking the commitment and by pointing out that the alignment they want cannot be reached in the abstract.

> Sajjad, you said last time that if this works, Trivium becomes a full-time thing, and that forces decisions about where you spend your life. I understand that and I don't want to be glib about it.
>
> But I'd push back on the sequencing. You're trying to decide whether to commit to Trivium before you know whether the market responds. That decision doesn't get easier by thinking about it harder. It gets easier when you're looking at three real conversations in your inbox that came from a post you wrote. Then it decides itself.
>
> And the way we've structured this makes the bet small. $3,000 a month, rolling. No lock-in. If in month two you hate it, you tell us and we stop. We recommend three months because that's what it takes for the founder arc to land, but we don't tie anyone in. We'd want that freedom ourselves.

Then the forcing question, directly at both of them:

> So let me ask you both, since you're both here. What specifically do the two of you need to agree on that you can't agree on right now, on this call?

Then stop and let it sit. Do not fill the silence. Whatever comes back is the actual deal blocker and you handle it live. Nine times out of ten there is nothing, and the silence itself makes that obvious to them.

### 3. "What if the leads come and we can't service them?"

> Two things. First, that's the good version of this problem, and you're a strategic finance firm. You'll solve it.
>
> Second, timing works in your favour. Founder arc content doesn't produce inbound in week two. In your segment it's more like eight to twelve weeks before you see real conversations. You told Mustafa you want to hire one or two engagement managers onshore. That's roughly the same timeline. Start now and the pipeline arrives when the capacity does. Start when the capacity is there and you've bought yourself another three months of dead air.

### 4. Haseeb's employer risk

He solved this himself. Give him the credit and formalise it.

> Haseeb, you're at an Insight-backed company, one below VP, and you'd rather not raise this with them. Your instinct last time was right. Sajjad is the face. You're not posting a personal brand. Trivium posts, Sajjad writes, you repost and comment where it's natural. Zero incremental exposure for you. If your situation changes later, we optimise your profile then and it's a separate conversation. It's not part of this decision.

### 5. "Sajjad is spread across four things"

> Your time cost is one 45 to 60 minute call every two weeks, which is a podcast interview more than a meeting, plus approvals in WhatsApp. That's it. Everything else is on us. Compare that to what writing twelve posts a month yourself would cost you, which is why it hasn't happened in four months.
>
> And the spread is an asset here, not a problem. Wall Street, Dinar Standard, Saudi, the family office, GuardianCare, the finance platform, the governance platform. Most founders we work with have one story. You have seven. That's a year of content before we repeat ourselves.

### 6. Price pressure

Hold the number. If they push, trade rather than discount.

> I'm not going to move on $3,000, and I'll tell you why. It's already positioned to be an easy yes for a firm your size, and if I cut it now you'd rightly wonder what the real number was. What I can do, if it helps your cash flow planning, is take the first three months in one payment so it's a single line item rather than a recurring one. But the monthly is the monthly.

### 7. "Send it over and we'll come back to you"

Pre-empted by the frame, but if it comes:

> I hear you, and I'd rather not do that, because that's what happened after the first call and after the second. You've got the deck. You've got the analysis. Nothing new is going to arrive in your inbox that changes the decision. So let me ask it a different way. Put the paperwork aside. Do you want to do this or not?

---

## 42-52: The close

Ask explicitly. Do not soften it into a question about next steps.

> Alright. I've said everything I've got. Sajjad, Haseeb, do you want to work with us?

On yes, move immediately and physically. Do not congratulate them for thirty seconds while the moment cools.

> Great. I'm going to send two things right now while we're on the call. The agreement and the payment link. Give me twenty seconds.

Send both. Then:

> Haseeb, the agreement's in your inbox from Sajjad's side too. It's two pages, rolling monthly, thirty days' notice either way. Have a read now and shout if anything's off. Sajjad, the payment link is for the first month. First charge today, then same date monthly.

Watch them do it. Getting the card in during the call is the entire difference between a closed deal and a warm one. If they say they'll do it after, ask what's blocking it now, and if the answer is finance approval, get a named date and a WhatsApp commitment from Haseeb specifically since he's the owner.

## 52-60: Book onboarding and set the next seven days

Do not end the call without this in a calendar.

> Last thing and then I'll let you go. The onboarding call is 90 minutes and it's the only long call we'll ever do. Sajjad, it's mostly you. Your upbringing, New Jersey, why you left Wall Street, Dinar Standard, the fourteen years in Saudi, what you're building now. Clients often say it feels like therapy. It's how we get the raw material for the next six months.
>
> I've got Thursday 8am, Monday 8:30am, or Tuesday 8am your time. Which one?

Then confirm what happens between now and then:

> Before that call we'll send a short questionnaire and ask for a few photos. On the call we'll cover the admin so nothing spills into email. Profile optimisation goes out within a week of onboarding, first batch of content a few days after that. Nothing publishes without your sign-off.

Get it into the WhatsApp group before you hang up.

---

## If they still won't sign

Never leave with an open-ended follow-up. Two acceptable outcomes only.

**A named blocker with a date.** "What has to be true for this to be a yes?" Get the specific thing, name who owns it, put a call in the diary before you leave the call. If they can't name a blocker, that's a no, and treat it as one.

**A clean no.** Take it well and say so. Samad introduced you and these people move in the same circles as half your pipeline. A gracious no from you is worth more than a grudging maybe from them. Ask one question on the way out: "For my own learning, was it the price, the timing, or you just don't want to be that visible?" The answer is worth having.

---

## Things not to do on this call

Don't re-teach the arcs. Mustafa did it on 1 July, you did it on 22 July. A third time signals you have nothing new and gives Sajjad space to intellectualise.

Don't introduce Haseeb as a second paid seat. It's real money later and a terrible idea today. New options create new things to discuss internally. Close one seat, revisit at month three.

Don't get pulled into the riba and Islamic finance debate. Haseeb flagged trolling risk, Sajjad has strong views and mentioned Omar Suleiman. It is a genuinely interesting conversation and it will eat twenty minutes. Park it: "That's a real question and it's exactly what we'll work through in onboarding. Today I want to keep us on the decision."

Don't discount to get the yes. They are a finance firm. A price that moves under mild pressure tells them what the service is worth.

Don't let Sajjad take the call from his car.
